---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import EmailCapture from '../components/EmailCapture.astro'

const helps = [
  {
    title: 'Real career-pivot stories',
    body: 'The actual jobs, dead ends, and lucky breaks that add up to a career change — not a polished highlight reel.',
  },
  {
    title: 'How to break into sports with no connections',
    body: 'Where the real entry points are, and how to get noticed when you have no resume line to lean on.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Permission to take the long way',
    body: 'Proof that bartending, security shifts, and unglamorous detours can still add up to the work you actually want.',
  },
]
---

<Layout title="About - Garret James Hokr" description="A career-switcher's path from bartending to the sports industry.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-12 text-center">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Just a kid from New Jersey</h1>
    <div class="mt-8 mx-auto w-56 h-56 sm:w-64 sm:h-64 rounded-lg border border-gold-dark/20 overflow-hidden bg-[#e2d5b5]">
      <img
        src="/images/garret.png"
        alt="Garret James Hokr"
        class="w-full h-full object-cover scale-150 translate-y-[16%]"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pb-16">
    <div class="space-y-6 text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        My name is Garret James Hokr, and every week I write for people trying to break into the sports industry from the outside: no real experience, no meaningful connections, no obvious way in.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've built a dream career in sports. But I'll be honest: professional sports wasn't always the target. That came later, after a run of career switches, a lot of wrong turns, and the people I met along the way.
      </p>
      <p>
        The passion, though, goes back as far as I can remember. To one morning, really.
      </p>
      <p>
        Summer 2002. We'd finished an early Sunday dinner, sunlight still coming through the dining-room windows, when my mother reminded me we'd be up at 4:45 the next morning. I asked her why. She smiled and said, "Because tomorrow is the start of the World Cup."
      </p>
      <p>
        My mother was born in Colombia; my father is a New Jersey native with Neapolitan roots. Growing up in a big Colombian-Italian family taught me early that there was a whole world of countries and cultures beyond the New Jersey suburb I lived in. So when I sat down for that match (Brazil vs. Turkey, played in Ulsan, South Korea), I was gone.
      </p>
      <p>
        A South American powerhouse against a nation both European and Asian. Twenty-two players thousands of miles from home, a ball, some grass, and a stadium packed to watch them. Brazil won 2-1. But it wasn't the result that stayed with me; it was my mother, with no real dog in the fight, reacting like it was life or death. That something so simple could move people that deeply woke up a curiosity that still runs through everything I do.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'd go on to live in Arizona, Spain (twice), Portugal, and Colorado. Through my late teens and twenties I chased whatever I was curious about: barista, bartender, bouncer, travel agent, tech salesman on Wall Street. None of them had "sports" in the title. What held them together was the same curiosity about people: what they wanted, what they feared, how they saw the world. It never occurred to me that I had no destination in mind until the pandemic hit, and I realized I wanted to spend my life on the one thing I was purely passionate about: soccer.
      </p>
      <p>
        So at 27, with a good bartending job and steady money in New York, I left it.
      </p>
      <p>
        At 28, I was a student again: enrolled in a football-business master's in Lisbon, paying tuition in cash with six bar shifts a week, starting over from zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        Breaking in didn't come from a perfect résumé or a dream job posting. It came from volunteering at grassroots soccer events, following up after every rejection instead of going quiet, and taking a ticketing and inside-sales role at the Colorado Rapids that looked nothing like the dream on paper, but turned out to be the right room.
      </p>
      <p>
        At 29, I landed my first real role in professional sports. At 30, I was working for the exact organization, in the exact corner of the industry, I'd spent years trying to reach.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today, I run a nonprofit called the <a href="https://2631foundation.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-outline font-semibold text-gold-dark hover:text-gold-deep transition-colors">2631 Foundation</a>, built around the attention of the 2026 World Cup, the 2028 LA Olympics, and the 2031 Women's World Cup, so the kids growing up around those moments actually get to benefit from the sport, not just watch it happen around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        And I write this newsletter, because the path nobody tells you about (the detours, the rejections, the unglamorous jobs that mattered more than they looked) is the one that actually got me here. The choices that seem like the wrong turn are often the right room.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're trying to navigate a few careers, pivot into sports, and go all in on the dream, I'd love to have you along for the journey.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16 border-t border-gold-dark/20">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-center sm:text-left">How this newsletter helps you</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
      {helps.map((item) => (
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold">{item.title}</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-20 border-t border-gold-dark/20 text-center">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">One story, every week.</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-soft">Join the readers figuring out their own side quest.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 flex justify-center">
      <EmailCapture align="center" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
